-### [Laplacian-Based Deformation for Gaussian Splatting](22006-laplacian-based-deformation-for-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Deformation.
+*   **Rank-2 Degeneracy:** The covariance is rank-2, meaning the Gaussian is planar. If the normal vectors of two Gaussians are nearly parallel but they are offset along the normal, the offset calculation is sensitive to the tolerance $\epsilon$ [S1].
+*   **Optimization Gaps:** Minor gaps between adjacent Gaussian kernels arise from joint optimization of photometric and geometric objectives. The tolerance $\epsilon$ is critical to account for these gaps; setting it too low may break connectivity, while setting it too high may introduce spurious links [S1].
+*   **SpikingGS Dependency:** The method relies on the specific activation cut-off $\bar{V}_{ip}$ from SpikingGS. Using a different surface-aligned GS method requires adapting the definition of $\Omega_i$ accordingly [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Building the graph requires checking intersections for all pairs of $N$ kernels, leading to $O(N^2)$ pairwise checks. Each check involves geometric intersection tests between ellipses [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Stores the graph adjacency list, $O(N + E)$ where $E$ is the number of edges. The graph is sparse as only surface-adjacent kernels connect [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Eliminates spurious connections in noisy or non-manifold reconstructions by enforcing surface proximity [S1]. Enables direct application of Laplacian-based deformation techniques without proxy meshes [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Dependent on the quality of the surface-aligned reconstruction (e.g., SpikingGS). If the initial Gaussians are not well-aligned to the surface, the graph may be incorrect [S1]. Requires tuning of tolerance $\epsilon$ [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The graph construction requires checking intersections for all pairs of $N$ Gaussians, leading to $O(N^2)$ pairwise checks. Each check involves solving for the intersection of an ellipse and a line (or plane), which is constant time $O(1)$ but involves square roots and trigonometric functions.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^2)$ in the worst case for storing the adjacency matrix, though the graph is typically sparse.
+*   **Strengths:** It accurately captures surface topology by ignoring Euclidean proximity that does not correspond to surface connectivity. This is superior to standard point-cloud graphing for deformation and editing tasks on complex surfaces [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** It is computationally expensive due to the $O(N^2)$ pairwise checks and the complexity of the geometric intersection tests. It is specific to surface-aligned Gaussians (rank-2) and may not generalize directly to volumetric Gaussians without modification [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in "Proxy-Free Gaussian Splats Deformation with Splat-Based Surface Estimation" (2026) [S1]. It builds on surface-aligned Gaussian Splatting methods like SpikingGS [S1] to enable proxy-free deformation by constructing a Laplacian operator directly on the Gaussian kernels [S1].
+The method was introduced in "Proxy-Free Gaussian Splats Deformation with Splat-Based Surface Estimation" (2026) [S1]. It addresses the limitation of existing point-based distance metrics (Euclidean, Mahalanobis) which fail to reliably establish connectivity in challenging conditions where points are close in space but distant along the surface manifold [S1]. The approach builds upon SpikingGS [19] to leverage its superior surface alignment quality [S1].
 
